# Media playback and output selection

Help someone identify what is playing, choose a supported operation and understand which speaker or screen will change. Artwork supports recognition; it must not displace the track title, playback state or target name. Opening a card, navigating or changing a theme must never start playback or move audio to another device.

## Scope and native ownership

The native media_player domain supplies playback states and integration-dependent commands. The media browser only lists suitable players. This specification describes presentation and confirmation around those capabilities; it does not add a media adapter. [Official Home Assistant source](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/media_player/), reviewed 11 November 2026.

This page completes the design specification for **HA-GAP-016**. Its examples are synthetic documentation, not a claim that a new native feature or adapter has been installed. The application integration must meet the task-specific acceptance below.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Required presentation | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Playing / paused | Name the item and output; use Pause or Resume only when supported. | Do not infer paused from zero volume. |
| Idle / off / on | Keep idle, powered off and on-without-playback-detail distinct. | Do not call every non-playing state stopped. |
| Buffering | Show Preparing playback without advancing a confirmed position. | Do not display an invented progress percentage. |
| Unknown / unavailable | Explain the missing state beside the player name. | Keep retained artwork clearly separate from current playback. |
| Live or unknown duration | Use Live stream or Duration not supplied as appropriate. | Do not render an invalid seek range. |

## Integration contract

1. Read supported commands, source list, volume bounds, media duration and position from the actual integration. A source selector requests a change; it is not ordinary navigation.
2. Separate the selected player from its input source. Do not infer multi-room grouping, casting or transfer support from the presence of several players.
3. Use the native service path and permissions. Keep a command pending until its documented acknowledgement, then use reported state for a playback result. A rejected request must retain the previous reported state.
4. Only extrapolate elapsed playback where the native model supports it. Reset interpolation on pause, source change or connection loss; never persist an estimated position as a new reading.

## Accessible interaction

- Give play, pause, mute and source controls visible names or equivalent accessible names containing the output where needed. Do not rely on changing icons alone.
- Volume and seek need names, bounds, keyboard adjustment and value text such as 35% or 2 minutes 10 seconds of 5 minutes. Retain the native keyboard contract.
- Preserve album/provider artwork rights. Use empty alternative text when adjacent title/artist already supplies its meaning; avoid flashing visualisers and respect reduced motion.

## Failure and recovery

- If changing source is rejected, keep the selected source and explain the rejection without retrying playback automatically.
- If position is absent, retain the item and state but omit the seek control. An unavailable artwork image must not hide playback controls.
- If the connection drops during a request, say its result is unconfirmed; inspect reported playback after reconnect before offering another command.

